THE MEASURING BINARY WAS HOLDING THREE COPIES OF THE INPUT AND C'S WAS HOLDING
ONE — 18.87 MB OF THE PORT'S REPORTED PEAK AT 4 MP.

date:   2026-09-03
host:   r7900x — AMD Ryzen 7900X, x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu. HEAP is heaptrack;
        RSS is `/usr/bin/time -v` max RSS, median of 5. Harness
        `rust/tools/mem_peak.sh`.
trees:  base   = 01ef6f1f1 (main)
        mecand = base + the one-byte `MeCandidate` commit
                 (benchmarks/mem_mecand_2026-09-03.meta)
        harn   = mecand + this commit
data:   benchmarks/mem_harness_2026-09-03.tsv

**THIS IS A HARNESS CHANGE AND MUST NOT BE READ AS AN ENCODER RESULT.** The
encoder allocates exactly what it allocated before; every byte this removes
belonged to `examples/perf_encode` itself. What it buys is that the two sides
of every port-vs-C memory number now hold the SAME amount of input, so the
ratio is about the encoders.

=================== WHAT WAS WRONG ============================================

`perf_encode` held, simultaneously, for the whole encode:

  y / u / v   the generated source planes                6.29 M at 4 MP
  yuv         a whole-sequence concatenation, built only
              so `std::fs::write` could take one slice    6.29 M per frame
  frames      one OWNED (fy, fu, fv) per frame, whose
              frame 0 was a `to_vec()` COPY of y/u/v      6.29 M per frame

= 12.58 M on the still arm, 18.87 M on the 1-frame videokey arm, **31.45 M on
the 2-frame inter arm**. `tools/perf_c_encode` reads the `.yuv` into ONE buffer
and feeds slices of it: 6.29 M / 12.58 M for 1 / 2 frames. So every port-vs-C
memory ratio this repo has ever printed carried an 6.3-18.9 MB handicap that
was the harness's, and `benchmarks/mem_heaptrack_2026-09-03.meta` accounted for
only 6.29 M of it ("the harness growth CANCELS in the arm difference").

The fix is two lines of shape: stream each frame's three planes to the `.yuv`
through a `BufWriter` as it is produced instead of concatenating, and MOVE the
caller's planes into frame 0 instead of cloning them. One copy remains — the
`frames` vector the encoder actually reads, which is what C's driver holds.

The `.yuv` bytes are unchanged (C's `.obu` is byte-identical before and after
on all 12 cells, which is the control: C reads the file this code writes).

